<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maryland Toleration Act</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1649</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> Toleration Act, passed by the colony's assembly in April 1649, guaranteed freedom of worship to Christians who believed in the Trinity. Promoted by the Catholic proprietor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-calvert">Cecil Calvert</a>, it protected the Catholic minority among a growing Protestant population, though it threatened death for anyone denying the divinity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. Repealed in 1654 and later restored, it is an early landmark of religious toleration in America.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maryland-toleration-act.jpg" alt="Maryland Toleration Act" width="320" height="122" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Assembly of the Province of Maryland · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Large_Broadside_on_the_Maryland_Toleration_Act.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
